  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{7\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
